  "description": "Compute canonical scaled dot-product attention mapping query, key, and value matrices to contextualized representations.\n\nDividing by sqrt(d_k) prevents dot products from growing excessively large for large dimensions, which would push softmax gradients into regions of vanishingly small derivatives.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{Attention}(\\mathbf{Q}, \\mathbf{K}, \\mathbf{V}) = \\text{Softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{Q} \\mathbf{K}^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\mathbf{V}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute pairwise similarity scores Q K^T divided by scaling factor sqrt(d_k)\n2. Apply numerically stable row-wise softmax normalization\n3. Multiply attention probability distribution by value matrix V and round to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list of shape (N, d) representing attended context vectors.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Scaled Dot-Product Attention\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **Scaled Dot-Product Attention** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nDividing by sqrt(d_k) prevents dot products from growing excessively large for large dimensions, which would push softmax gradients into regions of vanishingly small derivatives. In this problem, the goal is to: compute canonical scaled dot-product attention mapping query, key, and value matrices to contextualized representations.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Attention}(\\mathbf{Q}, \\mathbf{K}, \\mathbf{V}) = \\text{Softmax}\\left(\\frac{\\mathbf{Q} \\mathbf{K}^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) \\mathbf{V}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute pairwise similarity scores Q K^T divided by scaling factor sqrt(d_k)\n2. **Phase 2**: Apply numerically stable row-wise softmax normalization\n3. **Phase 3**: Multiply attention probability distribution by value matrix V and round to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
